---
import { getCollection, render } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Logo from '../components/Logo.astro';
import Wordmark from '../components/Wordmark.astro';

const entries = await getCollection('docs', ({ data }) => !data.draft);

const sections = await Promise.all(entries.map((e) => render(e)));

// Sidebar: one group per `#` part of the README (Manifesto, Framework,
// Using talaia, Contributing), each listing its `##` sections. Derived
// from the rendered headings, so renaming a section never breaks the
// grouping; only a `##` before the first `#` would end up ungrouped.
const toc = [] as { label: string; anchor: string; items: { anchor: string; title: string }[] }[];
for (const { headings } of sections) {
  for (const h of headings) {
    if (h.slug === 'footnote-label') continue;   // the footnotes' own (hidden) h2, not a section
    if (h.depth === 1) toc.push({ label: h.text, anchor: h.slug, items: [] });
    else if (h.depth === 2 && toc.length) toc[toc.length - 1].items.push({ anchor: h.slug, title: h.text });
  }
}

// Where "Talk to us" leads. No contact channel is decided yet: until it
// is, the button stays on the Companies tab.
const contact = '#companies';
---
<Base
  title="talaia — human in charge"
  description="A human-in-charge accountability standard for work done with AI: a certificate a person signs, and a method — Brief → Build → Watch → Sign — that makes the signature honest."
  toc={toc}
>
  <header class="hero" slot="hero">
    <div class="hero-in">
      <div class="lockup"><Logo size={30} /><Wordmark /></div>
      <h1 class="tagline">You direct. The AI builds.<br />Your name answers for it.</h1>
      <p class="hero-sub">Talaia is a human-in-charge standard for work done with AI, and
        its whole claim fits in one line: the machine may produce the work;
        only a person can answer for it. It gives that person a certificate to sign — under a
        published post, at the foot of a report, in a commit — and a
        method that makes the signature honest.</p>
      <p class="hero-cta">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href={`#${toc[0]?.anchor}`}>Read the manifesto</a>
        <a class="btn" href={`#${toc[4]?.anchor}`}>Apply it, solo or as a team</a>
        <a class="btn" href="#companies">Bring talaia to your company</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <article class="readme">
    {sections.map(({ Content }) => <Content />)}
  </article>
  <!-- Companies: the one body written here and not in the README, so
       the standard on GitHub and in llms.txt carries no commercial part. -->
  <section id="companies" class="co" aria-label="Companies">
    <p class="co-over">For companies</p>
    <h2 class="co-title">AI across the company, with a name on every piece of work.</h2>
    <p class="co-lede">Your teams already work with AI. What a customer, an auditor or
      your own board will ask is who read the result and who answers for it. The
      Certificate of Understanding gives every piece of work that answer,
      and <span class="wm">talaia</span> helps you adopt it across the organization, prove it,
      and ask the same of others.</p>

    <h3>One practice, three directions</h3>
    <p>The certificate is the same two clauses a single developer signs. Adopted by a
      company, it becomes a good practice that travels with the work.</p>
    <ul class="co-rows">
      <li><strong>Inside the company</strong> Every piece of work done with AI — code, a
        report, a post — carries the name of a person who read it in full and answers
        for it. The names accumulate as the work goes up: the author, the reviewer, the
        board.</li>
      <li><strong>To your customers</strong> Deliver the certificate as a seal on what
        you hand over: an AI helped make this, and a named person stands behind it.</li>
      <li><strong>From your distributors</strong> Require it of those whose work you
        receive, the way you already require any other standard of quality.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Official certification</h3>
    <p>The standard is open and free: any company can
      adopt <span class="wm">talaia</span> and say so, and that is its own word. Official
      certification is ours. <span class="wm">talaia</span> checks how the company works
      and puts its name behind the result, so that your customers do not have to take
      your word for it.</p>

    <h3>Services</h3>
    <p>You pay for help and for verification, never for the right to use the
      standard. Take one, or all four on the way to certification.</p>
    <ul class="co-cards">
      <li><strong>Training</strong> For the people who direct the AI and sign for its
        work: how to brief it, how to read what comes back, and when not to sign.</li>
      <li><strong>Audit</strong> An independent reading of where you stand today: which
        work carries a signature, which does not, and what stands in the way.</li>
      <li><strong>Consulting</strong> The certificate fitted to how you already work —
        your processes, your tools, your definition of done. Nothing you have is
        replaced.</li>
      <li><strong>Forward deployed engineers</strong> Our engineers inside your teams,
        putting the framework to work in your own repositories and pipelines until it
        runs without us.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="co-cta">
      <h3>Bring <span class="wm">talaia</span> to your company</h3>
      <p>Tell us how your teams work with AI today, and we will tell you what it takes
        to put a name on every piece of it.</p>
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href={contact}>Talk to us</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
